  "editorReview": {
    "text": "This is a biography that treats engineering as a game of institutional power, and its core argument—that authority is built through narrative control as much as technical skill—is exceptionally well-supported, particularly in its dissection of the professional credit dispute. The book excels at embedding the human cost of power within cold operational details, avoiding sentimentality. However, the reading experience is occasionally disrupted by unmarked chronological jumps between chapters and some repetitive, mechanical phrasing that can momentarily pull the reader out of the immersive histor",
